#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""pre-push: run the gates the pushed change can actually affect — concurrently — then report per-phase.

Four gates, in dependency order, so a change that would fail CI cannot leave the machine:

  1. `catalog.py build` — the actual markdown->HTML + mermaid render. This catches BUILD failures the
     test suite structurally cannot: a notation marker that doesn't head its block, a bad mermaid fence,
     an unregistered `index-def` slug — anything that aborts the render (rc=1). `catalog_tests.py` checks
     ALREADY-BUILT HTML, so it never sees a build abort. This is the gap this hook was strengthened to
     close: a `--no-verify` commit (which skips the pre-commit build) shipped a book-build abort straight
     to CI's "Render markdown -> HTML" step. Now the render runs at pre-push, on `main`, where mmdc is
     installed. (260729 RCA: a mid-block `index-def` aborted CI's build while `--tier1` stayed green.)

  2. `book/build_book.py --pdf --no-split` — the print-native LaTeX render + whole-book content-integrity
     gate (page floor/ceiling, title + chapter/part titles present, tail present, part-opener spread
     sensor). This is the SAME step CI runs on every push (.github/workflows/pages.yml), so a LaTeX
     compile error or a content-integrity regression is caught on the machine, not three minutes into
     CI — and the gitignored /book/mage-book.pdf refreshes on every push instead of going stale between
     renders. `--no-split` because the per-section review PDFs are a LOCAL REVIEW AID, not a gate: CI
     never compiles them (`_pdf_split_enabled` turns them off under CI), `build_book.py`'s own dispatch
     comment declares "the deploy pre-push gate [is] full-only", and pre-push may only hold a subset of
     what CI holds. Measured 261029 under fleet load: `--pdf` with splits 954s, `--pdf --no-split` 293s
     — the 21 section compiles were ~11 minutes of a 26.9-minute serial gate while gating nothing CI
     gates. Regenerate them on demand with `python3 book/build_book.py --pdf`.

  3. `handbook/scripts/build.py pdf` — the supplementary Software Engineering Handbook's LaTeX PDF
     render (parity with the book PDF). CI renders + publishes it on every push and asserts it
     fatal-if-missing, so a handbook render failure would fail CI; catch it here instead. This also
     refreshes the gitignored local review artifacts (the handbook PDF + the landers staple the pdf
     target emits), so they never go stale between manual handbook builds.

  4. `catalog_tests.py --tier1` — the fast, stdlib, deterministic checks: schema, link + anchor
     resolution, the book/*.html tracking (orphans), the concept-model drift lints (L1/L2/L3), the
     empty-<th> twin, skill bundle integrity. ~seconds.

Plus, when the push touches it:

  5. The workbench's own gates — the typecheck, UX-I1 affordance parity, the node unit tier, the
     bundle build, and then the three BROWSER-FACING tiers (smoke / browser / a11y) that serve
     `workbench/dist/` to a real Chromium. Those three used to run only in CI, which made a defect
     only a browser can see invisible before push BY CONSTRUCTION; on 261005 that cost one red CI
     run and left an a11y tab-order pin red on main for a day. ~147s measured, skip-if-absent on the
     Chromium and axe-core trees this hook does not own. See the block itself for the numbers.

     The block needs Node >= 22 (`.nvmrc` pins 24). Where PATH's `node` is older — which on this
     machine is the DEFAULT, nvm's default alias being v20.17.0 — the hook searches nvm's installed
     versions for one that clears the floor instead of standing down, because a gate skipped by
     default is a gate that does not exist. It skips only when there is genuinely no such Node.

  6. `workbench/validate.py --self-test` and a validation pass over every `workbench/**/*.mage.yaml`
     — the workbench's schema/semantics gate and its architectural assertions. A commit that lets the
     semantic kernel depend on a view fails here.

The wiring is held mechanically: `workbench/test/gate-reachability.test.ts` walks THIS FILE as one of
its three axes, against a gate set derived from `workbench/package.json`. A gate added to the manifest
and forgotten here fails a test instead of a push.

## Scope selection — why some gates are skipped

The two LaTeX renders dominate wall-clock, and a push that touches only `workbench/`
cannot change either PDF. So the hook reads the pushed commit range and runs the expensive renders
only when their own inputs moved.

The rule is deliberately asymmetric: **run everything unless the change set is provably disjoint from
a gate's inputs.** A wrong guess therefore costs time, never correctness. Specifically:

  - The deterministic floor (gate 4) ALWAYS runs. It is seconds, and it is the net.
  - `catalog.py build` ALWAYS runs. It is the render gate, and nearly every tree feeds the site.
  - The book PDF runs when book, model, shared LaTeX, or gate configuration inputs moved.
  - The handbook PDF runs when handbook, shared LaTeX, bibliography/font, or gate inputs moved.
  - The landers staple runs iff a path under `course/` moved AND `handbook/` did not (the handbook
    PDF target emits the staple itself, so scoping it on course/ alone would double the work).
    Added 261015: the staple is derived from course landers but had been policed by the handbook
    scope only, so a lander-only push left it stale and nothing noticed. A derived artifact must be
    scoped on the tree its INPUTS live in, not on the tree whose build happens to emit it.
  - The workbench gates run iff a path under `workbench/` moved — ALL of them, including the three
    browser-facing tiers. The scope guard is per-TREE and not per-tier on purpose: `workbench/dist/`
    is bundled from the whole of `workbench/src/`, so any source change can move what a browser
    sees, and a finer guess would be the first thing to be wrong.
  - If the range cannot be determined (a new branch, a shallow clone, an unreadable ref), EVERY gate
    runs. Unknown scope is never treated as empty scope.

## Concurrency — how the gates run without stepping on each other

Measured 261029 on the full book+handbook scope, the gates are mutually independent except where an
artifact flows between them, so they run as PHASES on a small worker pool (3 by default — this
machine routinely carries an agent fleet; `GC_PREPUSH_JOBS`, clamped to 1..4, tunes it). The checks
themselves are unchanged: same commands, same scope rules, same failure texts. Only the schedule moved.

The dependency edges, each from a measured artifact flow — not guessed:

  - `site build` -> `book PDF`: both emit the writing-style exports/routers
    (`_emit_writing_style_export` runs in the web build AND in `build_pdf`), so they must not
    overlap; and the PDF phase must read the projections the build refreshed.
  - `site build` -> `handbook PDF`: the handbook reads the shared bibliography surfaces the build
    refreshes; this also preserves the hook's historical ordering for the two renders.
  - `site build` + `book PDF` -> `tier1`: the deterministic floor reads the freshly built HTML,
    `book/web/`, `plugin/` (site build) and runs the caption-orphan sensor against
    `book/mage-book.pdf` when present (book PDF). Running it earlier would grade a stale artifact.
  - Everything else is pairwise write-disjoint: the toolchain check compiles in
    `latex/build/ci-tagging`, the TikZ suite in `latex/build/tikz`, the book in `book/latex/build`,
    the handbook in `handbook/generated` + `handbook/dist`, the workbench in `workbench/`.

The ONE shared mutable resource is the LuaLaTeX font cache (`latex/build/.texmf-var`, via
`MAGE_TEXMF_CACHE` in `tools/latex_build.py`). Concurrent compilers racing one luaotfload cache can
corrupt it, so each concurrent LaTeX phase gets its own PERSISTENT sibling cache
(`.texmf-var-<phase>`), seeded by an APFS clone of the main one the first time so no phase pays the
cold font-database rebuild. The book PDF — the canonical render — keeps the default cache, which is
safe because every other user of it is either ordered before it (site build compiles no TeX) or
redirected to its own cache.

Each phase's output is BUFFERED and emitted as one labelled block with its wall-clock seconds when
the phase finishes — never interleaved, because this repo has repeatedly lost a verdict to reading
the wrong fragment of a log. On the first red phase the hook stops launching, terminates the phases
still in flight (their status prints as CANCELLED, never as a verdict), and the LAST thing printed
is the failing phase's name and its original failure text. A summary table closes every run either
way, so the gate reports measurable results rather than just doing its work.

`GC_PREPUSH_PLAN=1 git push` (or feeding the hook its stdin by hand) prints every phase and the exact
argv of every step it WOULD run for this push's scope, without executing any of them — the mechanical
enumeration used to diff the executed check set across hook revisions.

Override: `GC_PREPUSH_ALL=1 git push` forces every gate regardless of scope.
Bypass entirely (humans, rare — e.g. pushing a WIP branch): `git push --no-verify`.

CI still runs `catalog_tests.py --full` (Tier-2: html-validate + sampled axe + `claude plugin validate`)
as the authoritative gate, with every stage unconditional — CI is the backstop that does not guess.
Install once with `python3 catalog.py install-hooks` (sets core.hooksPath=hooks).
No shell — pure Python, per the repo's tooling posture.
"""
import os
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
import time
from concurrent.futures import FIRST_COMPLETED, ThreadPoolExecutor, wait

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
from _hook_root import committing_toplevel

ROOT = committing_toplevel()

# Prefix -> the gate whose inputs live under it. A path matching none of these affects only the
# site, which gate 1 and gate 4 already cover unconditionally.
SCOPES = {
    "book": ("book/", "book-models/", "latex/", "tools/latex_build.py", "tools/pdf_structure.py",
             "tools/mage_latex.py", "tools/mage_assets.py", "tools/mage_tikz_charts.py", "tools/mage_tikz_review.py", "tools/mage_tikz_links.py", "tools/test_mage_tikz_links.py", "tools/mage_tikz_typography.py", "tools/test_mage_tikz_typography.py", "tools/tikz_audit.py", "tests/test_tikz_audit.py", "tests/fixtures/tikz/", "tools/mage_cover.py", "tools/cover_assets.py", "tools/install_texlive_ci.sh", "tools/check_print_toolchain.py", "hooks/", ".github/workflows/pages.yml"),
    "handbook": ("handbook/", "tools/cover_assets.py", "latex/", "tools/latex_build.py", "tools/pdf_structure.py",
                 "tools/install_texlive_ci.sh", "tools/check_print_toolchain.py", "book/references.bib", "book/fonts/", "book/typst/fonts/",
                 "hooks/", ".github/workflows/pages.yml"),
    "workbench": ("workbench/",),
    # The landers staple is DERIVED from course/lectures/**/index.md, but until 261015 its only
    # refresh path was a side effect of `handbook/scripts/build.py pdf` under the handbook scope.
    # A lander-only push therefore left dist/course-landers-stapled.md stale with no gate noticing
    # — and the staple is what a human reads. The join is now held where its inputs live.
    "course": ("course/",),
}

ZERO = "0" * 40
PLAN = bool(os.environ.get("GC_PREPUSH_PLAN"))


def _jobs() -> int:
    try:
        n = int(os.environ.get("GC_PREPUSH_JOBS", "3"))
    except ValueError:
        n = 3
    return max(1, min(4, n))


def _git(*args: str) -> str | None:
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(["git", *args], cwd=ROOT, capture_output=True, text=True, check=True)
    except (subprocess.CalledProcessError, OSError):
        return None
    return out.stdout


def _changed_paths() -> set[str] | None:
    """Paths in the pushed range, or None when the range cannot be established.

    git hands a pre-push hook one line per ref on stdin:
        <local ref> <local sha> <remote ref> <remote sha>
    A remote sha of all-zeros means the remote has no such branch yet, so there is no range to
    diff against and we must assume everything is in scope.
    """
    if os.environ.get("GC_PREPUSH_ALL"):
        return None
    try:
        lines = [ln.split() for ln in sys.stdin.read().splitlines() if ln.strip()]
    except OSError:
        return None
    if not lines:
        # Invoked without stdin (a manual run, or a git that gave us nothing) -- do not guess.
        return None
    paths: set[str] = set()
    for parts in lines:
        if len(parts) != 4:
            return None
        _, local_sha, _, remote_sha = parts
        if local_sha == ZERO:          # branch deletion: nothing to build
            continue
        if remote_sha == ZERO:         # new branch: no base to diff against
            return None
        out = _git("diff", "--name-only", f"{remote_sha}..{local_sha}")
        if out is None:                # unreadable ref (e.g. remote sha not fetched)
            return None
        paths.update(p for p in out.splitlines() if p)
    return paths


def _touches(paths: set[str] | None, prefixes: tuple[str, ...]) -> bool:
    if paths is None:
        return True
    return any(p.startswith(pre) for p in paths for pre in prefixes)


def _texmf_env(tag: str) -> dict[str, str]:
    """A per-phase LuaLaTeX cache so concurrent compilers never race one luaotfload database.

    Persistent across pushes (it lives beside the main cache), seeded by an APFS clone of the warm
    main cache on first use so the phase does not pay the cold font-database rebuild the shared
    cache exists to avoid. Falls back to a plain copy off APFS, and to an empty directory when
    there is nothing to clone — correctness never depends on the seed, only warmth does.
    """
    env = dict(os.environ)
    base = os.path.join(ROOT, "latex", "build", ".texmf-var")
    cache = os.path.join(ROOT, "latex", "build", f".texmf-var-{tag}")
    if not PLAN and not os.path.isdir(cache):
        if os.path.isdir(base):
            cloned = subprocess.run(["cp", "-c", "-R", base, cache],
                                    capture_output=True).returncode == 0
            if not cloned:
                shutil.rmtree(cache, ignore_errors=True)
                try:
                    shutil.copytree(base, cache)
                except OSError:
                    shutil.rmtree(cache, ignore_errors=True)
        os.makedirs(cache, exist_ok=True)
    env["MAGE_TEXMF_CACHE"] = cache
    return env


# ── The phase machinery: buffered output, one worker pool, terminate-on-first-red ──────────────────

_CANCEL = threading.Event()
_PROCS: set[subprocess.Popen] = set()
_PROCS_LOCK = threading.Lock()
_PLANNED: list[tuple[str, list[str]]] = []


class _Cancelled(Exception):
    pass


class _PhaseCtx:
    """One phase's world: run steps, buffer their output, stop on cancellation."""

    def __init__(self, name: str) -> None:
        self.name = name
        self.buf: list[str] = []

    def echo(self, line: str) -> None:
        self.buf.append(line)

    def run(self, argv: list[str], cwd: str | None = None, env: dict[str, str] | None = None) -> int:
        if PLAN:
            _PLANNED.append((self.name, list(argv)))
            return 0
        if _CANCEL.is_set():
            raise _Cancelled()
        proc = subprocess.Popen(argv, cwd=cwd or ROOT, env=env,
                                stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True)
        with _PROCS_LOCK:
            _PROCS.add(proc)
        # Close the start/cancel race: a queued phase can begin BETWEEN the cancel flag being set
        # and the terminate sweep reading _PROCS (observed in the 261029 sabotage run — a cancelled
        # handbook render ran 200s past the red). Registered first, then re-checked, so one side
        # always sees the other.
        if _CANCEL.is_set():
            try:
                proc.terminate()
            except OSError:
                pass  # already gone; termination is best-effort cleanup
        try:
            out, _ = proc.communicate()
        finally:
            with _PROCS_LOCK:
                _PROCS.discard(proc)
        if out:
            self.buf.append(out.rstrip("\n"))
        if _CANCEL.is_set():
            raise _Cancelled()
        return proc.returncode

    def py(self, *args: str, env: dict[str, str] | None = None) -> int:
        return self.run([sys.executable, *args], env=env)


class Phase:
    def __init__(self, name: str, fn, deps: tuple[str, ...] = (), cost: float = 1.0) -> None:
        self.name, self.fn, self.deps, self.cost = name, fn, deps, cost


def _emit_block(name: str, status: str, seconds: float, buf: list[str]) -> None:
    rule = "─" * 78
    sys.stderr.write(f"{rule}\npre-push phase: {name} — {status} ({seconds:.1f}s)\n{rule}\n")
    for chunk in buf:
        sys.stderr.write(chunk + "\n")
    sys.stderr.flush()


def _execute(phases: list[Phase]) -> int:
    """Run the phases on a bounded pool, respecting deps; first red terminates the rest.

    Only this (main) thread prints, so blocks never interleave. A failing phase's text is emitted
    the moment it lands AND repeated at the very end, because the last lines are the ones a human
    under a 20-minute gate actually reads.
    """
    by_name = {p.name: p for p in phases}
    status: dict[str, str] = {}
    seconds: dict[str, float] = {}
    failures: list[tuple[str, str]] = []  # (phase, failure message)

    def worker(phase: Phase):
        ctx = _PhaseCtx(phase.name)
        t0 = time.monotonic()
        try:
            message = phase.fn(ctx)
            return phase.name, ("FAIL" if message else "PASS"), message, time.monotonic() - t0, ctx.buf
        except _Cancelled:
            return phase.name, "CANCELLED", None, time.monotonic() - t0, ctx.buf

    pending = dict(by_name)
    running = {}
    pool = ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=_jobs())
    t_start = time.monotonic()
    try:
        while pending or running:
            if not _CANCEL.is_set():
                ready = [p for p in pending.values()
                         if all(status.get(d) == "PASS" for d in p.deps if d in by_name)]
                for p in sorted(ready, key=lambda p: -p.cost):
                    running[pool.submit(worker, p)] = p.name
                    del pending[p.name]
            if not running:
                # Nothing runnable and nothing running: deps failed or everything was cancelled.
                for name in pending:
                    status[name] = "NOT RUN"
                    seconds[name] = 0.0
                pending.clear()
                break
            done, _ = wait(set(running), return_when=FIRST_COMPLETED)
            for fut in done:
                del running[fut]
                name, verdict, message, dt, buf = fut.result()
                status[name], seconds[name] = verdict, dt
                _emit_block(name, verdict, dt, buf)
                if verdict == "FAIL":
                    failures.append((name, message))
                    if not _CANCEL.is_set():
                        _CANCEL.set()
                        with _PROCS_LOCK:
                            for proc in _PROCS:
                                try:
                                    proc.terminate()
                                except OSError:
                                    pass  # already gone; termination is best-effort cleanup
    finally:
        pool.shutdown(wait=True)

    total = time.monotonic() - t_start
    sys.stderr.write("\npre-push summary:\n")
    for p in phases:
        sys.stderr.write(f"  {status.get(p.name, 'NOT RUN'):9s} {p.name:34s} {seconds.get(p.name, 0.0):7.1f}s\n")
    sys.stderr.write(f"pre-push: {total:.1f}s wall over {_jobs()} worker(s)\n")
    if failures:
        name, message = failures[0]
        sys.stderr.write(f"\npre-push: phase FAILED: {name}\n")
        sys.stderr.write((message or "") + "(or `git push --no-verify` to override).\n")
        return 1
    return 0


# ── Scope resolution + the serial front (projection checks guard every consumer below) ─────────────

changed = _changed_paths()
if changed is None:
    print("pre-push: scope UNKNOWN — running every gate", file=sys.stderr)
else:
    active = [name for name, pres in SCOPES.items() if _touches(changed, pres)] or ["site-only"]
    print(f"pre-push: {len(changed)} changed path(s); scope: {', '.join(active)}", file=sys.stderr)

# LaTeX is authoritative. Check its tracked Markdown compatibility projections before any
# consumer reads them. Never regenerate/stage manuscript files implicitly: the author runs
# `sync`, reviews the projection diff, and stages the intended files with the LaTeX edit.
# These two stay SERIAL and FIRST: they are sub-second-to-seconds, and every render below
# consumes the projections they certify.
for _source_tool in ("tools/mage_latex.py", "handbook/scripts/latex_source.py"):
    if PLAN:
        _PLANNED.append(("projection checks", [sys.executable, _source_tool, "sync", "--check"]))
        continue
    _projection = subprocess.run([sys.executable, _source_tool, "sync", "--check"], cwd=ROOT)
    if _projection.returncode:
        print(f"pre-push: LaTeX projection check FAILED. Run `python3 {_source_tool} sync`, "
              "review and stage the generated Markdown with its LaTeX source, then retry.")
        sys.exit(_projection.returncode)


# ── Phase bodies. Same commands, same failure texts as the serial hook; only the schedule moved. ──

def _phase_toolchain(ctx: _PhaseCtx) -> str | None:
    # Host TeX Live's year is insufficient: its tagging packages may predate CI's.
    # Exercise both real covers and the known-bad control with the frozen compiler.
    if ctx.py("tools/check_print_toolchain.py", env=_texmf_env("toolchain")):
        return "pre-push: frozen print toolchain/cover tagging check FAILED — push refused.\n"
    return None


def _phase_site_build(ctx: _PhaseCtx) -> str | None:
    ctx.echo("pre-push: catalog.py build (render gate — catches build aborts CI would hit) …")
    if ctx.py("catalog.py", "build"):
        return ("\npre-push: catalog.py build FAILED — push refused.\n"
                "The site does not render; fix the build error above ")
    return None


def _phase_book_static(ctx: _PhaseCtx) -> str | None:
    env = _texmf_env("tikz")
    if ctx.py("-m", "unittest", "tests.test_tikz_audit", env=env):
        return "pre-push: TikZ geometry and reviewed-layout regression failed"
    if ctx.py("tools/test_mage_tikz_links.py", env=env):
        return "pre-push: TikZ reference preservation regression failed"
    if ctx.py("tools/test_mage_tikz_typography.py", env=env):
        return "pre-push: TikZ semantic typography regression failed"
    # Pins the qpdf exit-code contract. Runs HERE as well as in CI precisely because the failure it
    # guards is CI-only: the pinned newer LaTeX emits duplicate /Group keys that make qpdf exit 3,
    # and this host's older TeX does not, so the real build goes green locally either way. The test
    # injects a fake runner, so it costs milliseconds and needs neither qpdf nor TeX.
    if ctx.py("tools/test_qpdf_repack.py", env=env):
        return "pre-push: qpdf repack exit-code contract regression failed"
    if ctx.py("tools/mage_tikz_charts.py", "--check", env=env) or \
            ctx.py("tools/mage_tikz_review.py", "--figures-only", env=env):
        return "pre-push: grayscale TikZ candidate validation failed"
    return None


def _phase_book_pdf(ctx: _PhaseCtx) -> str | None:
    ctx.echo("pre-push: book/build_book.py --pdf --no-split (LaTeX PDF + strict final-log warnings + content-integrity gate) …")
    if ctx.py("book/build_book.py", "--pdf", "--no-split"):
        return ("\npre-push: book/build_book.py --pdf FAILED — push refused.\n"
                "The PDF does not render or fails the content-integrity gate; fix the error above ")
    return None


def _phase_handbook_pdf(ctx: _PhaseCtx) -> str | None:
    ctx.echo("pre-push: handbook/scripts/build.py pdf (handbook LaTeX PDF + strict final-log warnings) …")
    if ctx.py("handbook/scripts/build.py", "pdf", env=_texmf_env("handbook")):
        return ("\npre-push: handbook/scripts/build.py pdf FAILED — push refused.\n"
                "The handbook PDF does not render; fix the error above ")
    return None


def _phase_staple(ctx: _PhaseCtx) -> str | None:
    ctx.echo("pre-push: handbook/scripts/build.py staple (landers staple — course/ moved) …")
    if ctx.py("handbook/scripts/build.py", "staple"):
        return ("\npre-push: handbook/scripts/build.py staple FAILED — push refused.\n"
                "The course-landers staple does not emit; fix the error above ")
    return None


def _phase_tier1(ctx: _PhaseCtx) -> str | None:
    ctx.echo("pre-push: catalog_tests.py --tier1 (deterministic gate) …")
    if ctx.py("catalog_tests.py", "--tier1"):
        return ("\npre-push: catalog_tests.py --tier1 FAILED — push refused.\n"
                "Fix the failing gate(s) above, ")
    return None


def _phase_workbench(ctx: _PhaseCtx) -> str | None:
    # The TypeScript gates run FIRST and are the cheap ones: a type error or a failing unit test is
    # a faster, more localized signal than a model-validation failure, and tsc under
    # noUncheckedIndexedAccess catches a class the tests cannot reach.
    #
    # npm/npx need Node >= 22 (.nvmrc pins 24). A developer whose shell has not run `nvm use` would
    # otherwise see a confusing failure from inside tsc rather than a clear one here, so the version
    # is checked up front and the gate SKIPS rather than failing — a missing toolchain is not a
    # broken change, and CI runs these unconditionally.
    #
    # BUT A SKIP IS A GATE THAT DID NOT RUN, and measured 261005 the skip was the COMMON case rather
    # than the rare one: this machine's login shell resolves `node` to nvm's default alias, v20.17.0,
    # so pushing without `nvm use` skipped every gate in this block — the three browser tiers wired in
    # the same day included. A gate whose precondition is false by default is the gap this hook was
    # just strengthened to close, wearing a different costume: nothing is unwired, and nothing runs.
    #
    # So the hook LOOKS for the Node it needs before giving up. `.nvmrc` says nvm, and nvm keeps every
    # installed version under `$NVM_DIR/versions/node/v<x.y.z>/bin` — a path a hook can read without
    # sourcing a shell function. The pinned version is preferred, then the highest that clears the
    # floor. Nothing outside that directory is searched and nothing is installed, so the fallback is
    # still a genuine skip: no nvm, or no version at or above the floor, and the block stands down.
    FLOOR = 22

    def _node_major(exe: str) -> int:
        try:
            out = subprocess.run([exe, "--version"], capture_output=True, text=True, check=True).stdout
            return int(out.lstrip("v").split(".")[0])
        except (subprocess.CalledProcessError, OSError, ValueError):
            return -1

    def _nvm_candidates() -> list[str]:
        """nvm's installed `bin` directories, the `.nvmrc` pin first, then newest major first."""
        root = os.path.join(os.environ.get("NVM_DIR") or os.path.expanduser("~/.nvm"),
                            "versions", "node")
        if not os.path.isdir(root):
            return []
        try:
            with open(os.path.join(ROOT, ".nvmrc"), encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                pin = fh.read().strip()
        except OSError:
            pin = ""

        def rank(name: str) -> tuple[bool, int]:
            try:
                major = int(name.lstrip("v").split(".")[0])
            except ValueError:
                major = -1
            return (bool(pin) and name.startswith(f"v{pin}."), major)

        try:
            names = sorted(os.listdir(root), key=rank, reverse=True)
        except OSError:
            return []
        return [os.path.join(root, n, "bin") for n in names]

    # The environment every command in this block runs under. Empty prefix => PATH's own Node is fine.
    wb_env = dict(os.environ)
    wb = os.path.join(ROOT, "workbench")
    node_ok = _node_major("node") >= FLOOR
    if not node_ok:
        for bin_dir in _nvm_candidates():
            if _node_major(os.path.join(bin_dir, "node")) >= FLOOR:
                wb_env["PATH"] = bin_dir + os.pathsep + wb_env.get("PATH", "")
                ctx.echo(f"pre-push: workbench gates using {bin_dir} — PATH's `node` is below "
                         f"{FLOOR} and this one is not")
                node_ok = True
                break

    if not node_ok:
        ctx.echo(f"pre-push: workbench gates SKIPPED — no Node >= {FLOOR} on PATH and none installed "
                 "under nvm (`nvm install` reads .nvmrc); CI runs them unconditionally")
    else:
        for label, args in (
            ("tsc --noEmit (strict + noUncheckedIndexedAccess + exactOptionalPropertyTypes)",
             ["npx", "tsc", "--noEmit"]),
            ("npm run check:parity (UX-I1: no capability with only one affordance)",
             ["npm", "run", "check:parity", "--silent"]),
            ("npm test (unit + parity + source hygiene)", ["npm", "test", "--silent"]),
            ("npm run build (the published bundle must actually bundle)", ["npm", "run", "build", "--silent"]),
        ):
            ctx.echo(f"pre-push: workbench {label} …")
            if ctx.run(args, cwd=wb, env=wb_env):
                return (f"\npre-push: workbench {label} FAILED — push refused.\n"
                        "Fix the error above ")

        # ── The browser-facing tiers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        #
        # They run HERE, after `npm run build`, because each one serves `workbench/dist/` to a real
        # Chromium: without the bundle they would load a page with no app and assert over an empty
        # shell. CI orders them the same way and hard-asserts the bundle before each.
        #
        # WHY THEY ARE HERE AT ALL, measured rather than assumed. Until 261005 these three tiers ran
        # only in CI, so a defect only a browser can see was invisible before push BY CONSTRUCTION —
        # and twice in one day that is exactly what happened. Session persistence broke the browser
        # tier's pristine-page assertion through shared `localStorage`; the RESET button it added
        # entered the toolbar's tab order and left the a11y tier's opening-tab-order pin RED ON MAIN
        # for the rest of the day. Both changes reported their gates honestly — "tsc, node, parity,
        # build" — and those are precisely the gates this hook reached. A tier CI runs and the local
        # gate does not makes every push a coin flip on that tier.
        #
        # THE COST, measured on this machine at 261005 and the reason the list stops where it does:
        #   check:parity    0.3s
        #   test:smoke      7.6s   two served pages boot, no fallback strings
        #   test:browser   14.9s   167 tests, one Chromium
        #   test:a11y     124.0s   axe over four states, §19's thirteen operations by keyboard,
        #                          WCAG F6, and a generated drive per declared navigation path
        # ~147s added, against a gate that already exceeds twenty minutes cold on a push that moves
        # the book. The duration matters more than it looks: this hook runs BEFORE git transfers, so
        # every minute here ages both the verdict and the SSH credential (see CLAUDE.md's three push
        # failure modes). ~2.5 minutes bought the tier that held a live red for a day, which is the
        # trade; a tier costing ten would have earned a scoped subset instead.
        #
        # SKIP-IF-ABSENT, like the Node check above and for the same reason. Chromium comes from
        # `book/node_modules` and axe-core from the repo-root `node_modules` — neither is a dependency
        # of `workbench/package.json`, deliberately, because that tree is a symlink shared across
        # parallel agent worktrees. A developer who has not installed them has a missing toolchain,
        # not a broken change, and CI installs both unconditionally. The skip is LOUD and names the
        # install, because a silent skip is the failure this whole block exists to close.
        chromium = os.path.join(ROOT, "book", "node_modules", "puppeteer", "package.json")
        axe = os.path.join(ROOT, "node_modules", "axe-core", "axe.min.js")
        for label, args, needs in (
            ("npm run test:smoke (both served pages boot; no fallback string renders)",
             ["npm", "run", "test:smoke", "--silent"],
             ((chromium, "Puppeteer's Chromium (`npm ci` in book/)"),)),
            ("npm run test:browser (167 assertions over the served page in headless Chromium)",
             ["npm", "run", "test:browser", "--silent"],
             ((chromium, "Puppeteer's Chromium (`npm ci` in book/)"),)),
            ("npm run test:a11y (axe over four states + §19 by keyboard + every declared path)",
             ["npm", "run", "test:a11y", "--silent"],
             ((chromium, "Puppeteer's Chromium (`npm ci` in book/)"),
              (axe, "axe-core (`npm ci` at the repo root)"))),
        ):
            absent = [why for path, why in needs if not os.path.isfile(path)]
            if absent:
                ctx.echo(f"pre-push: workbench {label} SKIPPED — missing {'; '.join(absent)}; "
                         "CI runs it unconditionally")
                continue
            ctx.echo(f"pre-push: workbench {label} …")
            if ctx.run(args, cwd=wb, env=wb_env):
                return (f"\npre-push: workbench {label} FAILED — push refused.\n"
                        "Fix the error above ")

    ctx.echo("pre-push: workbench/validate.py --self-test (rules fire on models that violate them) …")
    if ctx.py("workbench/validate.py", "--self-test"):
        return ("\npre-push: workbench self-test FAILED — push refused.\n"
                "A semantic rule stopped detecting its own violation; fix the error above ")
    models = []
    for base in ("workbench/models", "workbench/examples"):
        d = os.path.join(ROOT, base)
        if os.path.isdir(d):
            models += [f"{base}/{n}" for n in sorted(os.listdir(d)) if n.endswith((".mage.yaml", ".mage.yml"))]
    for rel in models:
        ctx.echo(f"pre-push: workbench/validate.py {rel} …")
        if ctx.py("workbench/validate.py", rel):
            return (f"\npre-push: {rel} FAILED validation — push refused.\n"
                    "Shape, a numbered semantic rule, or an asserted architectural query; see above ")
    return None


# ── Assemble the plan for THIS push's scope. Same scope rules, same skip lines, as ever. ──────────

phases: list[Phase] = []
in_book = _touches(changed, SCOPES["book"])
in_handbook = _touches(changed, SCOPES["handbook"])

if in_book or in_handbook:
    phases.append(Phase("print toolchain", _phase_toolchain, cost=3))

phases.append(Phase("site build", _phase_site_build, cost=4))

if in_book:
    phases.append(Phase("book static checks", _phase_book_static, cost=2))
    phases.append(Phase("book PDF", _phase_book_pdf, deps=("site build",), cost=10))
else:
    print("pre-push: book PDF SKIPPED — no book or shared LaTeX input in this push", file=sys.stderr)

if in_handbook:
    phases.append(Phase("handbook PDF", _phase_handbook_pdf, deps=("site build",), cost=8))
else:
    print("pre-push: handbook PDF SKIPPED — no handbook or shared LaTeX input in this push", file=sys.stderr)

# The staple's inputs are course landers, so it is scoped on course/ — NOT on handbook/, which is
# where its only refresh used to hide. When handbook/ moved, the `pdf` target above already emitted
# it, so skip the duplicate work; the gate is cheap either way (no chapter build).
if _touches(changed, SCOPES["course"]) and not in_handbook:
    phases.append(Phase("landers staple", _phase_staple, cost=1))
elif _touches(changed, SCOPES["course"]):
    print("pre-push: landers staple already refreshed by the handbook PDF target", file=sys.stderr)
else:
    print("pre-push: landers staple SKIPPED — no course/ path in this push", file=sys.stderr)

if _touches(changed, SCOPES["workbench"]):
    phases.append(Phase("workbench gates", _phase_workbench, cost=5))
else:
    print("pre-push: workbench gate SKIPPED — no workbench/ path in this push", file=sys.stderr)

tier1_deps = ("site build", "book PDF") if in_book else ("site build",)
phases.append(Phase("tier1", _phase_tier1, deps=tier1_deps, cost=2))

if PLAN:
    for phase in phases:
        print(f"PLAN phase: {phase.name}" + (f" (after: {', '.join(phase.deps)})" if phase.deps else ""))
        phase.fn(_PhaseCtx(phase.name))
    for name, argv in _PLANNED:
        print(f"PLAN step [{name}]: {' '.join(argv)}")
    sys.exit(0)

sys.exit(_execute(phases))
